<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Shared build and packaging properties live in src/Directory.Build.props. Only what differs
    between the two packages belongs here.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <PackageId>Ank.DocToolkit</PackageId>
    <!--
      THE FIRST THING A CONSUMER READS on nuget.org, and nothing checks it. This text described a
      smaller library than shipped for four releases - it named only HTML to DOCX/PDF while
      Markdown import, the DOCX text exporters, XLSX export and PDF text extraction had all
      shipped. Exactly the failure that made docfx/guides/capabilities.md a GENERATED page, one
      surface earlier in a consumer's journey.

      Re-read this whenever a converter is added. `docfx/guides/capabilities.md` is the derived
      list to check it against - and check it PAIR BY PAIR, not by skimming for format names.

      That distinction is the second failure this text has had, found 2026-08-15 while working the
      Release-PR curation checklist on 0.27.3. Every format was named, so nothing looked wrong; but
      three of the eleven converters in the matrix - DOCX, XLSX and PPTX to PDF - appeared nowhere
      in the sentence. The first failure was an omission of whole capabilities and was obvious once
      read; this one was invisible to any reading that asked "is DOCX mentioned?" instead of "is
      DOCX to PDF mentioned?".

      D20's nuspec check proves this field is NON-EMPTY. Nothing can prove it is COMPLETE, which is
      why the checklist item stayed human - and why it took a person working that list to find it.
    -->
    <Description>
      Convert HTML and Markdown to DOCX and PDF; render DOCX, XLSX and PPTX to PDF; export DOCX to
      HTML/Markdown and XLSX to CSV/HTML; read text out of a PDF; and create/edit DOCX, XLSX and
      PPTX - pure managed, no native binaries, no browser, no LibreOffice, no Office interop. Runs
      on Linux, Windows and macOS, and makes no network calls at runtime.
    </Description>
    <!--
      Three groups, and the ORDER of thought matters more than the list. The old set was eight
      format nouns - docx;pdf;xlsx;pptx;html;openxml;document;conversion - which describe what the
      package is to somebody who already found it. Nobody in pain searches "openxml".

        1. Formats, kept: people do search these.
        2. The words normal people use (word, excel, powerpoint) and THE JOB they are doing
           (html-to-docx, docx-to-pdf, mail-merge) - the symptom that brought them here.
        3. The four constraints (linux, cross-platform, offline). These ARE the differentiator and
           not one of them was a tag.

      Dropped `document` and `conversion` as too generic to rank for, and `html` in favour of the
      more specific `html-to-docx`.

      DELIBERATELY NOT KEYWORD-STUFFED. nuget.org dilutes relevance across a package's tags, so
      twenty weak tags rank worse than ten strong ones. Adding a tag has a cost; keep this list
      short and argue for anything new.
    -->
    <PackageTags>docx;pdf;xlsx;pptx;word;excel;powerpoint;openxml;html-to-docx;docx-to-pdf;mail-merge;linux;cross-platform;offline</PackageTags>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="ClosedXML" Version="0.105.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="DocumentFormat.OpenXml" Version="3.5.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="HtmlToOpenXml.dll" Version="3.5.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="OfficeIMO.Excel.Pdf" Version="3.2.2" />
    <PackageReference Include="OfficeIMO.PowerPoint.Pdf" Version="3.2.2" />
    <PackageReference Include="OfficeIMO.Word.Html" Version="3.2.2" />
    <PackageReference Include="OfficeIMO.Word.Markdown" Version="3.2.2" />
    <!--
      PDF text extraction only. PDFsharp owns the document's STRUCTURE (pages, merge, rotate,
      metadata); PdfPig owns its CONTENT. Neither does the other's job - see the E6 decision.

      Apache-2.0, and unusually clean: zero transitive dependencies, no native payload, no sockets.
      Measured before adopting rather than taken from the package page.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="PdfPig" Version="0.1.15" />

    <!--
      PDF utilities (page count, merge, page extraction, metadata). PDFsharp owns the
      document's STRUCTURE; PdfPig, above, owns its CONTENT (text). Neither does the
      other's job - see the E6 decision. MIT, so the licence set for the two together is
      MIT plus PdfPig's Apache-2.0, not a single class.

      Measured before taking it on, with a publish probe built as an APP rather than a
      library (a library publish omits native assets and reports zero for everything):
      no .so or .dylib, no banned package, and no SixLabors reference - so the
      SixLabors.Fonts 1.x guard is untouched. Three transitive dependencies, all
      Microsoft.

      CORRECTION, 2026-08-14. This comment used to say "OfficeIMO.Pdf, already here, cannot
      serve this: it WRITES PDFs and cannot read one back". That was WRONG WHEN WRITTEN.
      OfficeIMO.Pdf 3.2.0 - already a dependency on the day that decision was made - ships
      PdfDocumentReader with TextByPage(...) returning IReadOnlyList<string>, which is the
      exact shape PdfEditor.ExtractText returns. Verified against the cached 3.2.0 package,
      so it is not something the later 3.2.2 bump introduced.

      The mistake was not checking the graph before reaching for a package: it asked whether
      a NEW dependency could be added safely, never whether the capability was already
      present. The same error was caught in the A19 row, which planned to add Markdig for
      something OfficeIMO already did.

      E7 CONCLUDED, 2026-08-14: PdfPig STAYS, and now for measured reasons rather than a false one.

      OfficeIMO.Pdf really can do the job. PdfDocument.Open(byte[]) opens an arbitrary PDF and
      .Read.TextByPage() returns one string per page. Compared side by side on the same documents,
      its output is equivalent to ours - it separates a heading from a following paragraph rather
      than running them together, which is the A26 defect and the reason ContentOrderTextExtractor
      was chosen over PdfPig's own cheaper page.Text. It splits pages correctly too. The only
      differences found were line endings (PdfPig \r\n, OfficeIMO \n) and a whitespace-only run
      that OfficeIMO drops.

      Kept anyway, on three grounds:

      1. INDEPENDENCE. OfficeIMO WRITES the PDFs this library produces. Reading them back with the
         same library would make a bug in its model self-consistent and invisible - which is
         precisely why the test suite keeps PdfProbe as a hand-rolled independent oracle instead of
         verifying OfficeIMO with OfficeIMO. Using a different implementation in production buys
         the same property for consumers.
      2. IT WOULD BE A BREAKING CHANGE. ExtractText has shipped since 0.24.0 returning \r\n
         separators. Switching flips them to \n, silently, for anyone splitting on them.
      3. THE COST IS SMALL. Apache-2.0, zero transitive dependencies, no native payload. Removing
         it would take the closure from 30 packages to 29 and leave SixLabors.Fonts as the only
         non-MIT entry - a tidier sentence in the notices, not a change in kind.

      Revisit only if PdfPig stops being maintained or its licence moves. The reason to keep it is
      independence, so "OfficeIMO can do it too" is an argument FOR the current design, not against.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="PDFsharp" Version="6.2.4" />

    <PackageReference Include="OfficeIMO.Word.Pdf" Version="3.2.2" />

    <!--
      Pinned deliberately, not incidentally. OfficeIMO.Word asks for SixLabors.Fonts
      [1.0.0, 3.0.0), and 1.x is Apache-2.0 while 2.x moved to the Six Labors Split License 1.0 -
      Apache-2.0 only below $1M annual revenue, commercial above. Letting NuGet float to 2.x
      would silently move this package off permissive licensing, which is its whole reason to
      exist. The exact-version bracket keeps that from happening quietly; see
      THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt and the DependencyGuardTests guard.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="SixLabors.Fonts" Version="[1.0.1]" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    README.md, THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt and the shared icon are packed by
    src/Directory.Build.props. LICENSE is packed by this project only, matching what has shipped
    since 0.1.0 - the extensions package has never carried one. That asymmetry is preserved here
    rather than quietly fixed, because changing a package's contents is a release-visible change
    and belongs in its own commit.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="..\..\LICENSE" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    TableRowFinder is internal - consumers use DocxEditor, never it directly - but its one
    dangerous behaviour, refusing to reach into nested tables, fails SILENTLY when it regresses:
    the document is still schema-valid, just wrong. Proving that end-to-end shows a bad document
    without showing which rows were chosen, so the test project gets direct access.
    Same trade the extensions package already makes for its internal service implementations.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="DocToolkit.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      Marks this assembly safe to trim. This attribute IS the claim: it is exactly what the MSBuild
      <IsTrimmable> property emits, and what the trimmer reads to decide an assembly's default
      action.

      Written as the attribute rather than the property on purpose, and the reason is not style.
      Setting <IsTrimmable>true</IsTrimmable> makes the SDK inject a Microsoft.NET.ILLink.Tasks
      PackageReference at the version bundled with THAT SDK: 8.0.22/10.0.1 on the machine this was
      written, 8.0.29/10.0.10 on the CI runner. Both projects commit a packages.lock.json that CI
      asserts in locked mode, so that entry breaks the build on any SDK patch difference between any
      two machines, permanently. Measured: it failed CI on the first push, NU1004 "the package
      reference Microsoft.NET.ILLink.Tasks version has changed from [10.0.1, ) to [10.0.10, )".

      It also has to live in the project file rather than src/Directory.Build.props. An
      AssemblyMetadata item declared there makes multi-target framework inference fail outright with
      NETSDK1013, "the TargetFramework value was not recognized" - measured, and a Condition on
      TargetFramework does not avoid it.

      What this costs: the trim ANALYZER no longer runs over src/ at build time, since it ships in
      that package. What replaces it is strictly stronger. ci.yml's trim-guard job trim-publishes
      tests/TrimProbe over the whole dependency closure, fails on any warning naming DocToolkit, and
      RUNS the trimmed binary. The analyzer only ever saw our own source; the guard also sees
      ClosedXML, OpenXML, HtmlToOpenXml and OfficeIMO. Mutation-verified: an unsafe reflection call
      added to PptxSlide.Titled fails that job.

      IsAotCompatible is set as an attribute too, for the same two reasons as IsTrimmable above -
      the property would pull in Microsoft.NET.ILLink.Tasks and break locked-mode restore across
      SDK patches, and an AssemblyMetadata item in src/Directory.Build.props fails framework
      inference. (Written "locked-mode restore" rather than with the flag's leading double hyphen,
      which XML forbids inside a comment - it failed the build once already.)

      IT IS SET ONLY BECAUSE IT IS NOW VERIFIED, 2026-08-14. This used to read "deliberately not
      set: nothing has verified it end to end, and an unverified compatibility claim is worse than
      an absent one" - which was the right call for as long as it was true. What changed is
      evidence, not opinion: ci.yml's aot-guard job native-AOT-publishes tests/AotProbe over the
      real dependency closure and RUNS it, and every capability asserts on its result rather than
      merely completing. Measured on the first green run, on main:

        docx ok (1979 bytes) | replace ok | pptx ok (4945) | xlsx ok (6280)
        html->docx ok (1951) | docx->pdf ok (52850) | pdf->text ok

      including PdfPig's CMap handling and OfficeIMO's font work, which are the two most
      reflection-dependent paths in the graph. The only AOT warning in the whole closure is
      ClosedXML's IL2090; none is attributable to DocToolkit, which aot-guard asserts separately.

      A publish that merely LINKS would not have earned this. AOT breaks at runtime, when a type
      resolved by name turns out not to be there - so "it ran and the output was correct" is the
      only evidence worth anything here. If aot-guard ever goes red, remove this attribute rather
      than weakening the job.
    -->
    <AssemblyMetadata Include="IsTrimmable" Value="True" />
    <AssemblyMetadata Include="IsAotCompatible" Value="True" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
